China is the third-largest country in the world by area (about 9.6 million km²). It lies in East Asia, stretching from the Pacific Ocean in the east to deserts and mountains in the west.

Borders: Shares land borders with 14 countries, including India, Russia, Mongolia, and Vietnam.

Terrain: Very diverse — the west is mountainous and high (Himalayas, Tibetan Plateau), while the east has fertile plains, rivers, and coastlines.

Mountains: Includes Mount Everest, the world’s highest peak, on the China–Nepal border.

Rivers: Major rivers include the Yangtze (longest in Asia) and the Yellow River, both crucial for farming and history.

Climate: Ranges from arid deserts in the north and west (Gobi, Taklamakan) to tropical in the south, with temperate regions in between.

Coastline: Over 14,000 km along the Pacific, home to major port cities like Shanghai, Guangzhou, and Tianjin.

👉 In short: China’s geography is marked by huge contrasts—high mountains, vast deserts, fertile plains, and long coastlines, making it one of the most geographically diverse countries on Earth.
